message.content:
On this lateral chest X-ray, there is blunting of the posterior costophrenic angle. The left hemidiaphragm is typically identified by the gastric air bubble located anteriorly and inferiorly; in this image, a gastric bubble is visible below the left hemidiaphragm contour. The blunted posterior sulcus lies posterior to the spine and appears continuous with the right hemidiaphragm contour, while the left hemidiaphragm maintains a sharper angle near the gastric bubble. Therefore, the effusion is on the right side.

Uncertainty: Lateral views alone can be ambiguous for laterality without a corresponding PA view, but the presence of the gastric bubble helps localize the left side, making the posterior blunting likely right-sided.

{"answer":"B. small right pleural effusion","reason":"Posterior costophrenic angle blunting is seen on the side opposite the gastric bubble, indicating a right-sided effusion."}

